              {
                "label": "Exercise 3",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "Write the following dialogues in the speech bubbles. One dialogue each of Anum and Aleeza is missing. Guess what that dialogue could be and write it in the speech bubbles. Anum said, “Hi, Aleeza. How was your trip to the zoo?” Aleeza said, “It was a happy and exciting trip.” Anum said, “Really? What animals did you see?” Aleeza said, “I saw five big brown monkeys. They were big and fat.” Anum said, “…?” Aleeza said, “…”",
                    "a": "Anum: “What else did you see at the zoo?” Aleeza: “I saw the lions, the elephants and the monkeys. I liked the monkeys best of all.” (Model answer — the two missing dialogues are for you to guess.)"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Exercise 4",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "Read the following story and complete it by filling the speech bubbles. The dialogues are given below in a jumbled order. Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Lion's dialogues: 1. Why should I let you go? 2. You have disturbed my sleep. 3. How can a small mouse help a big lion? 4. Now I'm going to eat you. Mouse's dialogues: 5. Please don't eat me. Please let me go. 6. I don't know but I promise I'll help you. 7. I'm sorry. I didn't see you. 8. I may be able to help you some time. Bubble (a) — the lion's first dialogue:",
                    "a": "2 — “You have disturbed my sleep.”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (b) — the lion's second dialogue:",
                    "a": "4 — “Now I'm going to eat you.”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (c) — the lion's third dialogue:",
                    "a": "1 — “Why should I let you go?”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (d) — the lion's fourth dialogue:",
                    "a": "3 — “How can a small mouse help a big lion?”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (e) — the mouse's first dialogue:",
                    "a": "7 — “I'm sorry. I didn't see you.”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (f) — the mouse's second dialogue:",
                    "a": "5 — “Please don't eat me. Please let me go.”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (g) — the mouse's third dialogue:",
                    "a": "8 — “I may be able to help you some time.”"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the number of the correct dialogue in the bubble. Bubble (h) — the mouse's fourth dialogue:",
                    "a": "6 — “I don't know but I promise I'll help you.”"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Exercise 5",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "Write the complete story in your notebook. You can add phrases like: ‘The lion said’ or ‘The mouse said.’",
                    "a": "The Mouse and the Lion\nOne day, a mouse was playing in the jungle. A lion was sleeping there. The mouse did not see it. It ran over the lion's foot. The lion woke and caught the mouse. He said, “You have disturbed my sleep.” The mouse said, “I'm sorry. I didn't see you.” The lion said, “Now I'm going to eat you.” The mouse said, “Please don't eat me. Please let me go.” The lion said, “Why should I let you go?” The mouse said, “I may be able to help you some time.” The lion said, “How can a small mouse help a big lion?” The mouse said, “I don't know but I promise I'll help you.” So the lion let the mouse go. A few days later the lion was caught in a hunter's trap. The little mouse saw this and tried to help the lion. With his sharp teeth he cut the rope and the lion was free. “Thank you, little mouse for saving my life,” said the lion. The mouse said, “You saved my life so I was able to save your life.”"
                  }
                ]
              }
